from (values
('127.510.d.9.D',3,'Changing spoken words from one language to another as the words are spoken is:','["editing", "grading", "interpretation", "translation"]'::jsonb,2,'Interpretation is oral.','original'),
('127.510.d.9.D',4,'Changing written text from one language to another is:','["interpretation", "grading", "editing", "translation"]'::jsonb,3,'Translation is written.','original'),
('127.510.d.10.A',3,'Different dialects can express the same idea with different words or grammar, while still being valid forms of:','["English", "art only", "math", "music"]'::jsonb,0,'Dialect variety.','original'),
('127.510.d.10.A',4,'Saying ''He is going'' in Standard American English and ''He be going'' in another dialect shows dialectal:','["error", "variation", "disorder", "disease"]'::jsonb,1,'Variation.','original'),
('127.510.d.10.B',3,'Speech and language patterns can vary by language, region, and:','["blood type", "height", "culture", "shoe size"]'::jsonb,2,'Variation sources.','original'),
('127.510.d.10.B',4,'A person who speaks two languages may mix them, which is called code:','["locking", "freezing", "breaking", "switching"]'::jsonb,3,'Code-switching.','original'),
('127.510.d.10.C',3,'African American English, Southern English, and Appalachian English are examples of American English:','["dialects", "disorders", "errors", "diseases"]'::jsonb,0,'Dialects.','original'),
('127.510.d.10.C',4,'Dialects have their own rules for sounds and grammar, so they should be treated as rule-governed language:','["defects", "varieties", "errors", "mistakes"]'::jsonb,1,'Dialects are rule-governed.','original'),
('127.510.d.10.D',3,'A speaker whose first language lacks a certain English sound may replace it with a sound from the:','["calendar", "clock", "heritage language", "weather"]'::jsonb,2,'Transfer.','original'),
('127.510.d.10.D',4,'A Spanish speaker may say ''eschool'' for ''school'' because Spanish words do not begin with:','["t sounds", "m sounds", "vowels", "s plus consonant"]'::jsonb,3,'Transfer patterns.','original'),
('127.510.d.10.E',3,'English language learners may show errors that reflect the rules of their first language, which are not signs of a language:','["disorder", "dialect", "gift", "skill"]'::jsonb,0,'Not disorder.','original'),
('127.510.d.10.E',4,'When assessing an English language learner, the SLP should evaluate skills in both English and the child''s:','["favorite game", "home language", "bus route", "school lunch"]'::jsonb,1,'Assess both languages.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.A',3,'The nerves and brain areas that carry signals for speech and hearing make up the peripheral and central:','["digestive systems", "skeletal systems", "nervous systems", "muscular systems only"]'::jsonb,2,'Neural basis of communication.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.A',4,'The part of the brain most involved in producing speech, located in the left frontal lobe, is called:','["the cerebellum", "Wernicke''s area", "the brainstem", "Broca''s area"]'::jsonb,3,'Broca''s area produces speech.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.B',3,'The physical property of sound that we hear as how high or low it is, measured in hertz, is its:','["frequency", "duration", "timbre", "intensity"]'::jsonb,0,'Frequency relates to pitch.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.B',4,'The intensity of a sound, which we perceive as loudness, is measured in:','["inches", "decibels", "hertz", "watts"]'::jsonb,1,'Decibels measure loudness.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.C',3,'Hearing loss caused by a problem in the outer or middle ear is called:','["central", "mixed only", "conductive", "sensorineural"]'::jsonb,2,'Conductive loss.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.C',4,'Hearing loss caused by damage to the cochlea or auditory nerve is called:','["conductive", "external", "temporary only", "sensorineural"]'::jsonb,3,'Sensorineural loss.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.D',3,'Children with untreated hearing loss often have delays in speech and:','["language", "vision", "weight", "height"]'::jsonb,0,'Hearing loss delays language.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.D',4,'Early identification of hearing loss is important because the first few years are critical for language:','["decline", "development", "retirement", "loss"]'::jsonb,1,'Early intervention.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.E',3,'Communication skills change across the lifespan, so comparing speech and language in children and older adults shows how they change over:','["size", "price", "time", "color"]'::jsonb,2,'Lifespan changes.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.E',4,'Older adults may have trouble hearing high-pitched sounds due to age-related hearing loss called:','["vertigo", "otitis", "tinnitus", "presbycusis"]'::jsonb,3,'Presbycusis.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.F',3,'Speech disorders involve how sounds are made, while language disorders involve understanding or using:','["words and sentences", "only volume", "only pitch", "only gestures"]'::jsonb,0,'Speech vs language.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.F',4,'A person with a hearing disorder may also have difficulty with speech and language because hearing supports:','["digestion", "communication", "breathing only", "balance only"]'::jsonb,1,'Related disorders.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.G',3,'Problems with the production of speech sounds, fluency, or voice are called speech:','["rules", "talents", "disorders", "styles"]'::jsonb,2,'Speech disorders.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.G',4,'Difficulty understanding or using words and grammar is called a language:','["strength", "preference", "talent", "disorder"]'::jsonb,3,'Language disorder.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.H',3,'When a family needs an interpreter, a barrier can arise when the interpreter does not know the right medical:','["terms", "songs", "maps", "colors"]'::jsonb,0,'Interpreter barriers.','original'),
('127.511.d.1.H',4,'Using a trained professional interpreter rather than a child family member helps protect accuracy and:','["volume", "privacy", "speed", "fashion"]'::jsonb,1,'Professional interpreters.','original'),
('127.511.d.2.A',3,'In children, a common cause of temporary hearing loss is fluid in the middle ear from ear:','["exercises", "haircuts", "infections", "meals"]'::jsonb,2,'Otitis media.','original'),
('127.511.d.2.A',4,'In adults, a common cause of hearing loss is long-term exposure to loud:','["light", "water", "air", "noise"]'::jsonb,3,'Noise-induced loss.','original'),
('127.511.d.2.B',3,'A stroke can cause a language disorder called:','["aphasia", "dyslexia", "asthma", "anemia"]'::jsonb,0,'Aphasia.','original'),
('127.511.d.2.B',4,'Damage to the brain from a head injury can cause problems with speech, language, and:','["hair growth", "memory", "eye color", "height"]'::jsonb,1,'TBI effects.','original'),
('127.511.d.2.C',3,'A child who has trouble pronouncing sounds beyond the typical age may have a speech sound:','["gift", "habit", "disorder", "hobby"]'::jsonb,2,'Speech sound disorder.','original'),
('127.511.d.2.C',4,'A common symptom of a language disorder in a child is limited:','["height", "hair", "appetite", "vocabulary"]'::jsonb,3,'Language symptoms.','original'),
('127.511.d.2.D',3,'A communication disorder can affect a person''s school performance, friendships, and:','["self-esteem", "eye color", "blood type", "height"]'::jsonb,0,'Impact on the person.','original'),
('127.511.d.2.D',4,'Adults with communication disorders may struggle at work because it is harder to share ideas and follow:','["seasons", "directions", "trends", "recipes"]'::jsonb,1,'Functional impact.','original'),
('127.511.d.2.E',3,'Different cultures may view communication disorders differently, so clinicians must show cultural:','["bias", "pride", "sensitivity", "rigidity"]'::jsonb,2,'Cultural variation.','original'),
('127.511.d.2.E',4,'A family that believes a delay will resolve on its own may wait longer to seek help, which shows how culture can affect:','["diet only", "weather", "height", "help-seeking"]'::jsonb,3,'Cultural attitudes.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.A',3,'A child who deletes final sounds, such as saying ''ca'' for ''cat,'' is showing a phonological:','["process", "talent", "gift", "accent"]'::jsonb,0,'Phonological process.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.A',4,'Speech sound disorders may involve articulation errors or a pattern problem in the child''s phonological:','["vision", "system", "posture", "diet"]'::jsonb,1,'Articulation vs phonology.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.B',3,'A therapy approach in which the clinician teaches a child to produce a target sound through repeated practice is:','["surgery", "hypnosis", "traditional articulation therapy", "medication"]'::jsonb,2,'Articulation therapy.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.B',4,'A treatment that targets patterns of sound errors, such as the cycles approach, is used for phonological:','["strength", "talent", "growth", "disorders"]'::jsonb,3,'Phonological treatment.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.C',3,'Stuttering may include repetitions, prolongations, blocks, and secondary behaviors such as:','["eye blinking", "whistling", "singing", "clapping"]'::jsonb,0,'Secondary characteristics.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.C',4,'Behaviors that people who stutter use to escape or avoid stuttering are called:','["vowels", "secondary behaviors", "phonemes", "primary sounds"]'::jsonb,1,'Secondary characteristics.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.D',3,'A treatment approach that teaches speaking with easy onset and slower speech to reduce stuttering is called fluency:','["printing", "stopping", "shaping", "reading"]'::jsonb,2,'Fluency shaping.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.D',4,'The Lidcombe Program is an evidence-based treatment for stuttering in:','["older adults only", "teenagers only", "infants only", "young children"]'::jsonb,3,'Lidcombe.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.E',3,'A hoarse or breathy voice is a problem with vocal:','["quality", "meaning", "spelling", "color"]'::jsonb,0,'Voice quality.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.E',4,'A voice that is too loud or too soft is a disorder of vocal:','["resonance", "volume", "pitch", "quality"]'::jsonb,1,'Volume.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.F',3,'Rest, hydration, and reducing vocal strain are part of a plan to treat voice disorders using vocal:','["speed", "color", "hygiene", "fashion"]'::jsonb,2,'Vocal hygiene.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.F',4,'A voice treatment plan should be based on evidence and should include a medical referral to an:','["coach", "dentist only", "chef", "ENT physician"]'::jsonb,3,'ENT referral.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.G',3,'Using wrong word order or leaving out word endings in a child''s sentences shows difficulty with syntax and:','["morphology", "phonology", "anatomy", "audiology"]'::jsonb,0,'Language form.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.G',4,'A child who struggles to understand questions and directions has a problem with receptive:','["voice", "language", "fluency", "hearing aids"]'::jsonb,1,'Receptive language.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.H',3,'Treatment that gives a child focused language practice in play and conversation is a language:','["vacation", "holiday", "intervention", "penalty"]'::jsonb,2,'Language intervention.','original'),
('127.511.d.3.H',4,'Treatment decisions for language disorders should be guided by research evidence, clinical expertise, and client:','["fashion", "luck", "hair color", "values"]'::jsonb,3,'EBP components.','original'),
('127.511.d.4.A',3,'When speaking with a client, a clinician should choose communication skills that fit the age and the:','["situation", "color", "price", "weather"]'::jsonb,0,'Situation-appropriate communication.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
